        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES: <br>
Observation of light flashes in the eyes of Apollo astronauts, which were determined to be caused by radioactive cosmic rays, renewed concern about potential radiobiological effects of high linear energy transfer   (LET) particles in space flight.   The documentation of high-LET particles in a variety of space flight conditions is an important factor in assessing potential hazards to humans in space.
<P>
APPROACH: <br>
The 9-day ASTP mission was flown between July 15 and July 25, 1975, at an altitude of 220 km and an inclination of 51.6 degrees.  High-LET particle radiation was recorded in passive dosimeters worn by the three U.S. Apollo-Soyuz Test Project astronauts.  The dosimeters consisted of thin films of plastic nuclear track detectors and a silver chloride (Gal) crystal.  The radiation is measured by counting the tracks made as the radioactive particles impact the crystal and scar its surface.
<P>
After each dosimeter was returned to Earth, the plastics were removed and processed in temperature controlled, mechanically stirred etching tanks.  Scanning and measurement of the tracks made in the plastics was done with an optical microscope at 430x magnification.  The AgCl crystals were removed, in a darkroom, from each dosimeter and immediately stabilized with yellow light for 30 seconds and placed in a light-tight box for storage.  Tracks were developed by decorating each crystal with ultraviolet (UV) light for 17.5 minutes.  The crystals were then scanned in red light at 650x magnification in a darkroom.  Only tracks which intersected both surfaces (i.e. spanning tracks) of a crystal were counted.  
<P>
RESULTS: <br>
Generally, near-Earth missions with high inclination orbits (versus low inclination orbits) receive a higher flux of high-LET particles because of the decreased absorption of particles in the atmosphere at higher inclinations.  Lunar missions normally receive higher radiation exposures than near-Earth missions due to the absence of shielding of radioactive particles by the Earth's atmosphere.
<P>
A total of 48 events were located in a 0.192 square centimeter area of the crystal, giving the fluence value of 250 tracks/cm2.  The flux recorded in the plastics and AgCl crystal was, respectively, 1.15 and 27.5 particles/cm2 per day.  Thus, there was higher particle registration sensitivity in the AgCl than in the plastics.  Comparison of results showed that the high-LET particle flux incident on Skylab astronauts was a factor 2.9 larger than ASTP, due to both the lower altitude orbit of ASTP (220 km compared with 435 km for Skylab) and the greater spacecraft shielding that ASTP had.
<P>
The main feature of the ASTP measurements was a sharp decrease in particle flux toward large LET values.  Radiobiological damage expected from particles with long range and large LET values can be expected to be quite severe.
<END>
        </div>

